1
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:20,532
In August of 2009, Dalia Dippolito arrived home to what looked like a murder scene.

2
00:00:21,232 --> 00:00:22,785
Police surrounded her house.

3
00:00:23,588 --> 00:00:25,785
Crime-scene tape blocked the entrance.

4
00:00:26,513 --> 00:00:29,194
And an officer told her that her husband, Michael...

5
00:00:30,207 --> 00:00:30,967
had been killed.

6
00:00:32,103 --> 00:00:33,278
Dalia broke down.

7
00:00:34,009 --> 00:00:35,513
But there was just one problem.

8
00:00:36,521 --> 00:00:37,775
Michael wasn't dead.

9
00:00:38,852 --> 00:00:40,081
There hadn't been a murder.

10
00:00:40,913 --> 00:00:43,331
And nearly everything Dalia was looking at...

11
00:00:44,271 --> 00:00:46,097
had been staged by police.

12
00:00:47,078 --> 00:00:50,004
Because investigators believed the woman they were consoling...

13
00:00:50,761 --> 00:00:53,865
had spent the previous days arranging the murder herself.

14
00:00:54,848 --> 00:00:57,215
And the man she thought she'd hired to do it?

15
00:00:58,052 --> 00:00:59,349
He was a police officer.

16
00:01:00,133 --> 00:01:04,491
To understand how police got here, you have to go back only a few months.

17
00:00:59,349 --> 00:01:03,387
Dalia and Michael Dippolito's relationship moved incredibly fast.

18
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:07,947
When they began seeing each other, Michael was still married.

19
00:01:08,596 --> 00:01:09,816
He eventually divorced.

20
00:01:10,477 --> 00:01:12,908
And just days after that divorce became final...

21
00:01:13,603 --> 00:01:14,545
he married Dalia.

22
00:01:15,603 --> 00:01:18,582
But Michael also came into the relationship with baggage.

23
00:01:19,571 --> 00:01:22,359
He had previously served prison time for fraud...

24
00:01:23,079 --> 00:01:24,477
and was still on probation.

25
00:01:25,481 --> 00:01:31,276
The two would later dispute exactly how much Dalia knew about his criminal history when they got together.

26
00:01:32,291 --> 00:01:34,594
But only about six months into their marriage...

27
00:01:35,434 --> 00:01:42,575
police received a call that turned their relationship into a criminal investigation.

28
00:01:43,529 --> 00:01:46,795
The person who went to police was Mohamed Shihadeh.

29
00:01:47,868 --> 00:01:50,469
Court records described him as Dalia's lover.

30
00:01:51,303 --> 00:01:52,490
And according to Mohamed...

31
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:55,623
Dalia was trying to have Michael killed.

32
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:58,884
That's obviously an enormous accusation.

33
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:02,023
So police didn't simply take his word for it.

34
00:02:02,718 --> 00:02:05,442
Mohamed began working as a confidential informant.

35
00:02:05,817 --> 00:02:08,982
And police started recording his conversations with Dalia.

36
00:02:02,023 --> 00:02:07,174
According to prosecutors, these weren't vague conversations about wishing Michael would disappear.

37
00:02:07,968 --> 00:02:09,725
They were discussing finding someone...

38
00:02:10,388 --> 00:02:12,013
who could actually kill him.

39
00:02:12,863 --> 00:02:16,377
And by this point, police had more than just Mohamed's story.

40
00:02:07,018 --> 00:02:08,855
They had recorded conversations.

41
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:11,840
And they had a twelve-hundred-dollar payment...

42
00:02:12,488 --> 00:02:14,416
from Dalia to Mohamed.

43
00:02:15,312 --> 00:02:19,810
But investigators wanted to know what Dalia would do when she was put face-to-face...

44
00:02:20,473 --> 00:02:23,676
with someone she believed could actually carry out the killing.

45
00:02:25,084 --> 00:02:26,187
So they arranged a meeting.

46
00:02:26,187 --> 00:02:28,164
The man they sent in was Widy Jean.

47
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,157
Jean was a Boynton Beach police officer.

48
00:02:31,611 --> 00:02:33,435
But Dalia wasn't supposed to know that.

49
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:34,720
To her...

50
00:02:35,543 --> 00:02:38,941
Jean was simply the man Mohamed had found to take care of Michael.

51
00:02:45,543 --> 00:02:49,171
And Jean made sure there was very little ambiguity about what he was offering.

52
00:02:50,343 --> 00:02:52,125
He discussed entering Michael's home.

53
00:02:52,739 --> 00:02:55,277
He discussed making the scene look like a burglary.

54
00:02:56,092 --> 00:02:57,532
And he discussed killing Michael.

55
00:02:57,840 --> 00:02:58,772
Most importantly...

56
00:02:59,563 --> 00:03:02,917
Jean explicitly gave Dalia a chance to back out.

57
00:03:03,579 --> 00:03:05,415
The consequences weren't hidden from her.

58
00:03:05,803 --> 00:03:07,340
He made the situation clear.

59
00:03:08,117 --> 00:03:11,272
And when he gave Dalia the opportunity to stop everything...

60
00:03:12,023 --> 00:03:13,695
she made her position very clear.

61
00:03:14,349 --> 00:03:17,408
If this had been a normal murder-for-hire investigation...

62
00:03:23,695 --> 00:03:26,958
that conversation alone would already be extraordinary.

63
00:03:27,728 --> 00:03:30,034
But then police did something unusual.

64
00:03:30,611 --> 00:03:33,321
Instead of simply arresting Dalia after the meeting...

65
00:03:34,342 --> 00:03:36,456
they decided to make her believe...

66
00:03:37,679 --> 00:03:39,198
the murder had actually happened.

67
00:03:40,387 --> 00:03:42,790
Michael was never in danger from the supposed hitman.

68
00:03:59,198 --> 00:04:01,414
Police knew exactly who that man was.

69
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:04,339
But they wanted to see what Dalia would do...

70
00:04:05,066 --> 00:04:06,997
if she believed the plan had succeeded.

71
00:04:07,824 --> 00:04:10,392
So officers removed Michael from the situation.

72
00:04:10,965 --> 00:04:14,620
Then they staged his home to look like an active homicide scene.

73
00:04:15,307 --> 00:04:17,200
Police vehicles surrounded the house.

74
00:04:17,669 --> 00:04:19,063
Crime-scene tape went up.

75
00:04:19,985 --> 00:04:21,527
Officers took their positions.

76
00:04:22,073 --> 00:04:22,822
And at the time...

77
00:04:23,776 --> 00:04:26,931
COPS was filming with the Boynton Beach Police Department.

78
00:04:27,704 --> 00:04:33,401
Dalia's attorneys would later argue that the show's presence influenced how police handled the sting.

79
00:04:34,218 --> 00:04:35,418
Police denied that.

80
00:04:36,456 --> 00:04:57,729
And in 2019, an appeals court found that COPS was not involved in the surveillance or investigation...

81
00:04:58,432 --> 00:05:02,707
until after Dalia had already taken the steps that completed the solicitation offense.

82
00:05:03,816 --> 00:05:05,163
When Dalia arrived home...

83
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:09,445
everything around her suggested that something terrible had happened.

84
00:05:10,525 --> 00:05:12,657
An officer told her Michael had been killed.

85
00:05:13,439 --> 00:05:14,310
Dalia cried.

86
00:05:14,970 --> 00:05:16,406
She collapsed into an officer.

87
00:05:17,227 --> 00:05:19,906
The entire scene had been designed to make her believe...

88
00:05:20,623 --> 00:05:23,991
that the man she'd met with had done exactly what they discussed.

89
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:26,825
But every officer standing around her...

90
00:05:27,432 --> 00:05:28,823
knew Michael was alive.

91
00:05:34,309 --> 00:05:35,756
And police still weren't finished.

92
00:05:36,348 --> 00:05:38,240
They asked Dalia to come down to the station.

93
00:05:39,163 --> 00:05:42,150
investigators already knew an enormous amount.

94
00:05:42,895 --> 00:05:44,347
They had Mohamed's information.

95
00:05:45,031 --> 00:05:46,809
They had the recorded conversations.

96
00:06:30,572 --> 00:06:31,762
They had the undercover meeting.

97
00:06:32,626 --> 00:06:34,893
They knew exactly what Dalia had been told.

98
00:06:35,615 --> 00:06:36,596
What they didn't have...

99
00:06:37,364 --> 00:06:38,474
was her explanation.

100
00:06:39,622 --> 00:06:42,004
So detectives began asking questions.

101
00:06:42,678 --> 00:06:46,213
And Dalia continued denying that she had arranged Michael's death.

102
00:06:46,923 --> 00:06:47,573
For a while...

103
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:51,105
investigators let her tell her version of events.

104
00:06:51,979 --> 00:06:53,534
Then they started challenging it.

105
00:06:54,015 --> 00:06:54,741
And eventually...

106
00:06:55,787 --> 00:06:59,103
detectives decided to remove any remaining ambiguity.

107
00:07:26,213 --> 00:07:27,516
They brought someone into the room.

108
00:07:28,209 --> 00:07:29,765
Someone Dalia supposedly...

109
00:07:30,345 --> 00:07:31,227
had never met before.

110
00:07:32,302 --> 00:07:33,358
It was Widy Jean.

111
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:36,416
The man she believed was a hitman.

112
00:07:37,383 --> 00:07:40,001
When investigators asked Dalia if she knew him...

113
00:07:40,588 --> 00:07:41,321
she denied it.

114
00:07:41,902 --> 00:07:44,570
And that's when the entire situation collapsed.

115
00:07:38,674 --> 00:07:39,673
Michael wasn't dead.

116
00:07:40,395 --> 00:07:41,148
The hitman...

117
00:07:41,710 --> 00:07:42,533
wasn't a hitman.

118
00:07:43,236 --> 00:07:46,610
And the conversations Dalia had spent days having privately...

119
00:07:47,397 --> 00:07:48,231
had been recorded.

120
00:07:49,414 --> 00:07:50,884
Even after the arrest, though...

121
00:07:51,966 --> 00:07:53,849
the story somehow got stranger.

122
00:07:48,231 --> 00:07:52,384
From jail, Dalia called the man police said she'd just tried to have killed.

123
00:07:53,918 --> 00:07:55,569
Michael had now heard the recordings.

124
00:07:56,238 --> 00:07:57,216
He had heard her voice.

125
00:07:57,790 --> 00:08:00,796
He knew exactly what investigators were accusing her of arranging.

126
00:08:01,754 --> 00:08:03,144
And during their conversation...

127
00:08:03,917 --> 00:08:05,078
he confronted her about it.

128
00:08:06,125 --> 00:08:09,698
Dalia continued denying that she genuinely wanted him dead.

129
00:08:10,281 --> 00:08:10,892
At this point...

130
00:08:11,686 --> 00:08:14,592
the evidence appeared almost absurdly straightforward.

131
00:08:15,288 --> 00:08:17,080
There were recorded conversations.

132
00:08:24,104 --> 00:08:25,687
There was an undercover officer.

133
00:08:26,198 --> 00:08:27,876
There was a staged murder scene.

134
00:08:28,530 --> 00:08:29,710
There was an interrogation.

135
00:08:30,687 --> 00:08:32,056
But what happened in court...

136
00:08:32,831 --> 00:08:35,178
would stretch this case out for years.

137
00:08:35,738 --> 00:08:39,574
Dalia's defense couldn't simply pretend the recordings didn't exist.

138
00:08:40,179 --> 00:08:41,515
So the argument became...

139
00:08:42,632 --> 00:08:47,193
that the recordings didn't mean what prosecutors said they meant.

140
00:08:35,178 --> 00:08:38,252
Dalia maintained that she never genuinely wanted Michael killed.

141
00:08:39,121 --> 00:08:44,981
Her defense argued that the bizarre conversations were connected to an attempt to create a fake video...

142
00:08:45,699 --> 00:08:48,009
or some kind of reality-TV opportunity.

143
00:08:48,654 --> 00:08:49,323
In other words...

144
00:08:49,878 --> 00:08:50,880
she said she was acting.

145
00:08:52,301 --> 00:08:56,033
Her attorneys also attacked the way police handled the investigation.

146
00:08:56,840 --> 00:09:02,477
They pointed to the fact that COPS was filming with the department while the sting was taking place.

147
00:09:03,254 --> 00:09:06,886
The defense argued that the show's presence influenced the operation.

148
00:09:07,663 --> 00:09:10,146
Police denied that characterization.

149
00:09:10,853 --> 00:09:16,713
And the appellate court later rejected the claim that COPS' involvement amounted to objective entrapment.

150
00:09:17,524 --> 00:09:19,720
Michael also denied Dalia's version...

151
00:09:20,894 --> 00:09:26,253
that the supposed murder plot had been some mutually understood acting exercise.

152
00:09:38,753 --> 00:09:40,811
Then, in 2011...

153
00:09:42,395 --> 00:09:47,540
a jury convicted Dalia Dippolito of solicitation to commit first-degree murder.

154
00:09:47,561 --> 00:09:49,631
She was sentenced to twenty years in prison.

155
00:09:50,377 --> 00:09:51,337
For most cases...

156
00:09:51,926 --> 00:09:53,644
that would have been close to the end of the story.

157
00:09:54,244 --> 00:09:55,495
But three years later...

158
00:09:56,369 --> 00:09:58,942
an appeals court threw the conviction out.

159
00:09:59,741 --> 00:10:02,548
Not because the undercover recordings had been proven fake.

160
00:10:03,343 --> 00:10:05,875
Not because the court decided Dalia was innocent.

161
00:10:06,604 --> 00:10:08,147
The conviction was reversed...

162
00:10:08,722 --> 00:10:10,765
because of problems with jury selection.

163
00:10:11,408 --> 00:10:13,811
The case had received enormous publicity.

164
00:10:14,449 --> 00:10:22,378
And during jury selection, prospective jurors were exposed to highly prejudicial information about Dalia while sitting together.

165
00:10:23,519 --> 00:10:28,467
The appeals court concluded that the trial court hadn't adequately protected her right...

166
00:10:29,186 --> 00:10:30,454
to an impartial jury.

167
00:10:31,155 --> 00:10:32,590
So despite the recordings...

168
00:10:33,295 --> 00:10:34,292
despite the sting...

169
00:10:34,970 --> 00:10:36,758
and despite the first guilty verdict...

170
00:10:37,448 --> 00:10:39,594
prosecutors had to do it again.

171
00:10:40,678 --> 00:10:43,859
In 2016, Dalia went back before another jury.

172
00:10:44,478 --> 00:10:46,787
Again, the undercover recordings were played.

173
00:10:47,479 --> 00:10:52,560
Again, prosecutors argued that they showed a genuine attempt to arrange Michael's murder.

174
00:10:53,447 --> 00:10:53,884
And again...

175
00:10:55,631 --> 00:10:58,753
the defense insisted that the case wasn't what it appeared to be.

176
00:10:59,231 --> 00:10:59,961
But this time...

177
00:11:01,244 --> 00:11:01,987
nobody won.

178
00:10:39,594 --> 00:10:41,638
The six-person jury split evenly.

179
00:10:42,229 --> 00:10:45,011
Three believed Dalia was guilty.

180
00:10:45,407 --> 00:10:47,167
Three did not.

181
00:10:47,658 --> 00:10:50,838
The judge declared a mistrial.

182
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:54,944
Which meant prosecutors were now looking at a third trial...

183
00:10:56,129 --> 00:10:57,578
over a case that had begun nearly eight years earlier.

184
00:10:58,691 --> 00:11:00,175
And in 2017...

185
00:10:57,578 --> 00:10:59,078
they tried Dalia again.

186
00:11:00,069 --> 00:11:02,497
This jury didn't need long.

187
00:11:03,786 --> 00:11:05,834
After roughly ninety minutes of deliberation...

188
00:11:06,773 --> 00:11:09,243
Dalia Dippolito was convicted again...

189
00:11:10,154 --> 00:11:10,696
of solicitation to commit first-degree murder.

190
00:11:20,696 --> 00:11:23,163
This time...

191
00:11:24,319 --> 00:11:28,562
she received a sixteen-year prison sentence.

192
00:11:29,374 --> 00:11:30,805
Florida's Fourth District Court of Appeal affirmed the conviction in 2019.

193
00:11:31,087 --> 00:11:32,358
The Florida Supreme Court declined jurisdiction.

194
00:11:33,103 --> 00:11:35,997
And the United States Supreme Court denied review.

195
00:11:36,985 --> 00:11:39,587
Dalia later pursued federal habeas relief.

196
00:11:40,347 --> 00:11:42,009
But in May of 2025...

197
00:11:43,077 --> 00:11:45,216
the federal district court denied her petition...

198
00:11:45,894 --> 00:11:48,278
and denied a certificate of appealability.

199
00:11:49,251 --> 00:11:50,784
After years of trials...

200
00:11:51,739 --> 00:11:52,420
appeals...

201
00:11:53,013 --> 00:11:53,617
cameras...

202
00:11:54,488 --> 00:11:55,229
recordings...

203
00:11:55,946 --> 00:11:58,283
and increasingly unusual explanations...

204
00:11:48,278 --> 00:11:50,397
the case kept coming back to the same place.

205
00:11:51,407 --> 00:11:52,459
One conversation...

206
00:11:53,261 --> 00:11:55,388
inside an undercover officer's car.

207
00:11:56,257 --> 00:11:59,029
Police gave Dalia an opportunity to stop.

208
00:11:59,756 --> 00:12:01,376
They made the consequences clear.

209
00:12:02,045 --> 00:12:03,504
And the man sitting beside her...

210
00:12:04,216 --> 00:12:04,942
wasn't a criminal.

211
00:12:05,670 --> 00:12:07,047
He was recording everything.

